Subject: canvas: an opt-in, profile-seeded step on every readback route

`--fingerprint-noise`, off by default. This is the only place in this fork
where coherence is knowingly traded away, which is why it is a switch and not a
behaviour, and why this header spends as much space on what it costs as on what
it does.

WHY AT ALL

Coherence and unlinkability are not the same goal, and canvas is where they
part company. A composed identity renders a page the way *this build on this
host* renders it, which is exactly right for "a page cannot tell this from a
real machine" -- and it means two profiles run from one machine produce the
same canvas hash. A site that stores that hash joins their sessions without
caring what `navigator.platform` said. Nothing in the catalogue closes that:
the bytes come out of Skia, ANGLE and the driver, and there is no second
rasteriser to draw them with.

So this is an operator's decision rather than the compositor's, and it is asked
for rather than assumed.

WITHOUT THE SWITCH, NOTHING MOVES

The gate is three conditions, all required: the switch is present,
`base::apostate::Profile::Get()` is non-null, and the profile carries a
non-empty `source_id()`. `--fingerprint=host`, a launch with no profile, and an
authored profile with no id therefore all fail closed to stock output. A
host-derived seed would be a host fact in the pixels, which is the failure this
project exists to avoid, so there is no fallback. The policy resolves once per
renderer and `Enabled()` returns false for the rest of the launch; every call
site below is then a compare-and-return.

The seed is `PersistentHash` over a NUL-separated domain string and the profile
id, two halves into one 64-bit value -- the `SyntheticDeviceSeed` construction
from `base/apostate/media_devices.cc`. Stable across builds, processes and
launches, so the same profile perturbs the same pixels on the next launch and
on another host.

THE POLICY, AND WHY RULE 5 STILL HOLDS

Per pixel, the key is the seed, the clean pixel, its clean 3x3 clamped
neighbourhood, and its position in the returned rectangle. From that hash, each
colour channel takes one of {-1, 0, +1} of its stored value; for a
floating-point channel that step is one unit in the last place, which is the
same statement about the stored value and is invisible to an eye. Alpha never
moves. Nothing consults a clock, a counter, an object identity, a readback
count, thread-local state, `PaintImage::ContentId` or `base::Rand*`.

Two consequences follow from the key rather than from care:

  - Two reads of one surface agree, so `coh.render-determinism` holds with the
    switch on. A returning visitor with the same profile gets the same bytes.
  - A 1:1 canvas-to-canvas copy reads back identically, because the key holds
    content and position and the copy preserves both.

A pixel whose 3x3 neighbourhood is one value is skipped, and a rectangle that
is one value throughout is skipped whole. That is not a nicety: a flat fill is
the one thing a page can predict without knowing anything about the machine,
and `coh.canvas-compressibility` measures exactly it -- a 60x30 single-colour
fill encoded to 262 bytes in stock Chrome and 406 in a browser injecting canvas
noise, 55% larger for identical drawing commands. Under this policy the flat
fill is byte-exact, so that check sees stock. What moves is the edges, which is
where a rasteriser signs its name anyway.

The hash is `NoiseHash` from `core/canvas_interventions/noise_hash.cc`, reused
as-is. `NoisePixels` beside it is not: it perturbs alpha, it changes a nonzero
solid fill (its own unit test asserts that), it assumes four 8-bit channels,
and it keys on a per-page token that no code in this tree produces. Each of
those four is a requirement here inverted.

THE ROUTES, AND WHY THERE IS NO ONE HOOK

The backing store is the source of truth and stays clean. Each route is
transformed once, at the point the caller sees bytes.

    getImageData (HTML and offscreen 2D)   the returned ImageData, after the
                                           clean readback
    toDataURL / toBlob / convertToBlob     a copy of the raster, before the
                                           encoder
    transferToImageBitmap                  provenance only; the pixels stay
                                           exact
    createImageBitmap of a canvas          provenance, consumed at the
                                           structured clone
    WebGL/WebGL2 readPixels to a view      the caller's typed array
    WebGL2 readPixels to a PIXEL_PACK      the bytes getBufferSubData returns
    WebGL-on-WebGPU CPU readPixels         the caller's typed array

A single choke point was the obvious design and it is wrong three ways, each
measured in this tree:

  - `ImageDataBuffer::Create` has six call sites, and three are not canvas:
    `core/html/media/html_video_element.cc:539`,
    `core/inspector/inspector_audits_agent.cc:65` and
    `modules/accessibility/ax_node_object.cc:4314`. A hook there would perturb
    a video poster, an inspector audit and the accessibility tree.
  - `ContextGL()->ReadPixels` is also how `DrawingBuffer` takes its own
    snapshots (`platform/graphics/gpu/drawing_buffer.cc:2012`). A hook there
    would perturb internal rendering and then perturb it again at the output.
  - The CPU `createImageBitmap` crop/scale path reads pixels
    (`platform/graphics/static_bitmap_image_transform.cc:195`) on its way to a
    result that is itself an output. Perturbing the intermediate applies the
    policy twice, and twice is a different answer from once.

So provenance travels instead of pixels. `StaticBitmapImage` carries one bool
saying "this is a canvas's page-visible output", set at
`transferToImageBitmap` and at `createImageBitmap` of a canvas, propagated by
the ImageBitmap-of-an-ImageBitmap constructor, and read in exactly one place:
`ImageBitmap::CopyBitmapData`, which is the single caller of
`StaticBitmapImage::CopyImageData` and is itself reached only from the V8
serializer. That is the whole byte egress of an ImageBitmap, measured. The bool
is not a JavaScript property, not a CDP field, and setting it does not change a
pixel, so rule 3 is intact: a page can see the effect of the switch, which is
the point, and cannot see the switch.

FORMAT COVERAGE, MEASURED FROM THE VALIDATORS

An RGBA8-only implementation would leave a clean route out, which is worse than
no implementation: one format perturbed and another exact is a contradiction a
page reads in two calls. So the accepted set was extracted from the two
`ValidateReadPixelsFormatAndType` overrides and diffed against the mapper:

    accepted types    15   handled 15   unhandled none
    accepted formats  11   handled 10   unhandled GL_ALPHA

`GL_ALPHA` carries nothing but alpha and is deliberately exact. The fifteen
types include the six packed ones -- 5_6_5, 4_4_4_4, 5_5_5_1, 2_10_10_10_REV,
10F_11F_11F_REV and 5_9_9_9_REV -- whose bitfields are the spec's layout read
least significant first, since GL numbers a packed component from the most
significant end unless the name says _REV. The shared exponent of RGB9_E5
scales all three mantissae at once, so it sits in the slot the policy never
touches. Pack state is honoured in full: PACK_ALIGNMENT row padding,
PACK_ROW_LENGTH, PACK_SKIP_PIXELS, PACK_SKIP_ROWS and the destination offset;
the rectangle is walked where GL put it.

On the canvas side `ImageData` is `unorm8`, `float16` and `float32`
(`core/html/canvas/image_data.cc:115-131`) and all three are handled, as are
the colour types a canvas resource provider produces -- N32 in both byte orders
and RGBA_F16 (`platform/graphics/canvas_2d_resource_provider.cc:590,595`) --
plus the 1010102 and norm16 variants. A colour type the policy does not
describe is left exact rather than guessed at.

THE PIXEL-PACK BUFFER

`readPixels` with a bound `PIXEL_PACK_BUFFER` returns nothing and writes GPU
memory, so there is no caller-visible byte at the call. Perturbing the buffer
is not an option: a GPU command may still consume it, and a fork that corrupts
what a shader reads is a bug rather than a policy.

So the readback records its geometry on the `WebGLBuffer` -- offset, width,
height, strides, format, type, and nothing else; no pixel value is held in the
renderer -- and `getBufferSubData` applies the policy to the copy it hands
back. It re-reads the tagged rectangle out of the buffer first, because the key
reads each pixel's 3x3 neighbourhood and a request that clipped the rectangle
would otherwise get a different answer for the same pixels. Only pixels the
request covers whole are replaced; a pixel cut in half by the requested range
has no perturbation of its own to report.

The record follows the bytes. `bufferData` clears it. `bufferSubData` drops the
rows a write touches and keeps whole rows above and below, splitting one record
into two where the write lands in the middle -- a rectangle half of whose bytes
came from script is not a readback. `copyBufferSubData` carries across only a
rectangle that moved whole. Untagged bytes are copied out exactly. Eight
records per buffer, oldest evicted, so a loop of readbacks cannot grow the list
without end; the evicted rectangle then reads back exactly, which is the same
answer the switch-off path gives.

WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER

`captureStream` and the canvas-to-video frame path are out of scope, stated
here rather than implied: `HTMLCanvasElement::CopyRenderingResultsToVideoFrame`
is a separate media egress with a frame-format policy of its own, and a page
can compare a captured frame against `getImageData` of the same canvas. The
WebGL-on-WebGPU backend's CPU read is covered; its PBO write goes to the driver
but that backend leaves `getBufferSubData` and `copyBufferSubData`
`NOTIMPLEMENTED`, so it has no script-visible buffer egress to tag yet, and
when one arrives it needs the WebGL2 treatment. Audio, client rects,
`measureText` and WebGL parameters are not touched, and neither is any image,
video frame or `ImageData` that did not come out of a canvas.

THE CHECK IT FAILS

Draw a scene; draw the same scene eight times larger; downsample and compare. A
perturbation keyed on a pixel and its immediate neighbours does not survive
being averaged with 63 of them, so the two images disagree in a way no single
machine's rasteriser does. Stock Chromium does not match itself exactly across
that test either, but it mismatches differently. That residual is inherent to a
per-pixel intervention rather than a defect in this one, and it is why the
switch is off by default: a profile is meant to be a machine, and this makes it
a machine with a policy.

The ones it passes, which is the set a detector actually runs: render twice,
read twice, known-pixel and solid-fill byte equality, majority vote across many
reads, cross-route agreement, and a returning visit on the same profile.

V1, AND A MEASURED BASELINE

Every translation unit this patch touches compiles on macos-arm64:
`base_rendering_context_2d.cc`, `apostate_readback_noise.cc`,
`webgl_readback_noise.cc`, `webgl_buffer.cc`, `image_bitmap.cc`,
`static_bitmap_image.cc`, `html_canvas_element.cc`, `offscreen_canvas.cc`,
`webgl_rendering_context_base.cc`, `webgl2_rendering_context_base.cc`,
`webgl_rendering_context_webgpu_base.cc` and `render_process_host_impl.cc`.
The last of those is also the check on `base/base_switches.h`, and
`static_bitmap_image.cc` the check on its header.

No V2, because this patch has not been built yet. What has been done is the
other half of the pair METHODOLOGY section 10 names: the harness was run
against the binary *without* the change, seven launches on one macos-arm64
host, headless with the software GL fallback, and it reports 25 checks passing
and exactly four failing -- the four that assert the switch did something. A
harness that passed on that binary would be testing nothing. Measured there, in
stock behaviour and therefore as the baseline this patch must not move:

  - a 64x32 single-colour fill encodes to 183 PNG bytes, and read back through
    `getImageData` is exactly rgb(37, 99, 235) in every pixel;
  - `captureStream` plus `grabFrame` returns the same digest as `getImageData`
    of the same canvas, which is the equality the out-of-scope note above
    predicts will break under the switch and stay broken;
  - the scale comparison already disagrees with itself without any policy in
    force: 5491 of 57600 bytes differ, maximum delta 162 per channel, a scene
    drawn at 1x against the same scene drawn at 8x and downsampled. So the
    residual that check finds is a change in an existing disagreement rather
    than a new one;
  - and one finding for an open row that is not this patch's: an OffscreenCanvas
    2D context created with `willReadFrequently: true` does NOT produce the same
    bytes as an `HTMLCanvasElement` 2D context for the same drawing operations,
    while the same offscreen canvas with default options does, through both
    `transferToImageBitmap` and `convertToBlob`. `coh.canvas-offscreen-parity`
    has been asking whether stock Chrome satisfies that relation since it was
    opened; on this host and this scene it does not, and the discriminator is
    the raster decision rather than the scope. Recorded on the row, still one
    host and one scene.

The driver is `/tmp/w1/NoiseReadback/verify.js` with `probe.html` beside it,
throwaway rather than part of the tree. It serves the page over loopback and
the page posts its own result back, so there is no CDP and no injected script.
Against the built binary it asserts: two launches of one seed identical, two
seeds different, switch-on different from a switch-off baseline taken from the
same binary, `--fingerprint=host` identical with and without the switch, a
solid fill byte-exact and still 183 bytes encoded, a 1:1 canvas copy reading
back identically, ten reads producing one value, every encode and clone route
agreeing with the reference for its own read depth, the PBO route agreeing with
the CPU route and surviving `bufferSubData` and `copyBufferSubData`, and a
non-canvas ImageBitmap untouched.

